  <Disclosure id="wave-4" title="A reported fourth wave, corrected twice, then assessed by Galaxy" note="pattern-matched; Galaxy suspects it but has not promoted it">

  <p>
    On 3 August 2026 the @intangiblecoins account published a pattern-matched
    fourth-wave claim while transactions were still pending. The
    <a href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-announcement/">opening post</a>
    states 218 transactions, 462 victim addresses, 216 fresh destinations and
    388.92748828 BTC across blocks 960,778 to 960,792. Its two linked Pastebins
    hold the <a href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-confirmed-pastebin/">confirmed-transaction address set</a>
    and a <a href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-pending-pastebin/">pending set</a>
    stated as another 104 sweeps, 199 victim addresses and 70.16757699 BTC,
    all signalling replace-by-fee. The original three-post thread, both lists
    and the account's two later corrections are held.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The original wave-4 post's transaction, address, amount and block-range figures, and the pending Pastebin's stated replace-by-fee set"
      source="The captured @intangiblecoins original thread posts and linked Pastebin pages, 3 Aug 2026"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-announcement/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    The first correction, posted at 01:37 UTC on 3 August, removed six of 216
    destination addresses because they had years of transaction history before
    block 960,183 on 30 July 2026, which the author judged unlikely for
    attacker addresses. Of the remaining addresses, 206 were stated to have
    been freshly created to receive the sweeps.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The removal of six of 216 wave-4 destination addresses for prior history, and the statement that 206 of the rest were freshly created"
      source="The first captured @intangiblecoins wave-4 correction, 3 Aug 2026"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-address-correction/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    The second correction, posted at 03:22 UTC and credited to Nunchuk, states
    that the circulated set erroneously included multisig addresses, while
    waves 1 to 3 contain none. It gives wave 4 as circulated at 857 addresses
    and 486.11 BTC, a multisig discount of 89 addresses and 20.58 BTC, and a
    surviving core of 709 addresses and 448.73 BTC. The author stresses that
    wave 4 rests on pattern matching, that no direct victim has confirmed a
    wave-4 loss yet, and that the word "likely" is used out of caution.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The second correction's multisig finding, its stated circulated, discounted and surviving wave-4 figures, and its statement that wave 4 rests on pattern matching without direct victim confirmation"
      source="The second captured @intangiblecoins wave-4 correction, 3 Aug 2026, crediting Nunchuk"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    Later on 3 August, at 22:51 UTC,
    <a href="/record/sources/glxy-wave4-caveat/">Galaxy published its own assessment of the wave</a>:
    medium-high confidence that wave 4 is substantially comprised
    of an attacker, a total of {fig('galaxyInclWave4Btc')} if it is included,
    and no promotion into the top-line numbers because no victim has confirmed
    inclusion. coldcard-hack-tracker carries the wave at 443.34 BTC across 703
    addresses, and states its own derivation for that figure: it starts from
    448.73 BTC, which it attributes to Galaxy rather than to @intangiblecoins,
    and drops six further destinations that already had prior on-chain history,
    a cut of 5.39 BTC. It also reads Galaxy's approximately 2,055 BTC ceiling
    as the 1,596 BTC high-confidence figure plus that 448.73 BTC candidate.
    Galaxy's own thread publishes neither 448.73 BTC nor any itemised wave-4
    component, so the reconciliation is the tracker's reading of Galaxy rather
    than Galaxy's own.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="Galaxy's medium-high-confidence wave-4 statement, and the tracker's 443.34 BTC, 703-address wave-4 line with its stated derivation from 448.73 BTC and its reading of Galaxy's 2,055 BTC ceiling"
      source="Galaxy Research's captured 3 August thread, which states 2,055 BTC and no wave-4 component, and the coldcard-hack-tracker captures of 4 Aug 2026, 03:05 UTC and 7 Aug 2026, 13:20 UTC, which carry the same wave-4 figures and derivation; the attribution of 448.73 BTC to Galaxy is the tracker's own"
      href="/record/sources/coldcard-hack-tracker/"
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    The stated figures do not reconcile from the correction posts alone: 857
    addresses minus the 89-address multisig discount gives 768, not the stated
    709, and 486.11 BTC minus 20.58 BTC gives 465.53 BTC, not the stated
    448.73 BTC.
    <Claim
      basis="derived"
      scope="The observation that the second correction's stated subtractions give 768 addresses and 465.53 BTC rather than its stated 709 addresses and 448.73 BTC"
      source="Integer subtraction of the counts and amounts stated in the second correction itself; no other input is used"
      href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/"
    />
    The post does not explain the difference. As with other conflicting
    figures on this page, the numbers are preserved as the source states them
    rather than silently adjusted.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wave 4 sits outside Galaxy's confirmed total and outside the 30 July sets
    above. Whether the wave-4 set belongs to the same
    incident is recorded as an open question <a href="#not-yet-known">below</a>.
  </p>

