  <Disclosure id="causation" title="Causation remains unverified">

  <p>
    The timing and transaction pattern are consistent with the public incident,
    but the blockchain does not identify the hardware or software that generated
    a private key. TFTC explicitly separates its reproduced chain event from the
    unproven claim that every transaction resulted from this firmware flaw.
    Galaxy's 3 August thread states that the 14 smaller footprints it
    identified could be many different attackers individually exploiting the
    now-known vulnerability.
    <Claim
      basis="unverified"
      scope="The share of the attributed transaction sets actually caused by the firmware flaw, and the site's use of the word theft, which records the public incident attribution rather than an established finding about authorisation: the transactions, amounts, timing and consolidation are documented in the captured sources above, but the chain does not show whether each transfer was authorised or who operated the sweep"
      source="The exact share caused by this firmware flaw has not been established from public evidence as of a 15 August 2026 recheck"
      href="/record/sources/tftc-who-must-move/"
    />
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

